          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em String phenomenology
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            based on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+particle+physics%29"} models
            that are derived or at least motivated from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            (as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFTs"} effective QFTs
            from string
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
          %p
            Broadly speaking,
            %em string phenomenology
            refers to investigations of the connection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            to experimentally observed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            reproduces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and/or the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
          %p
            String theory models naturally match the
            %em general
            conceptual structure of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            plus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (which is what drives the interest in string theory in the first place): for instance the standard model is a four dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            with a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} non-Abelian gauge symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family+of+elementary+particles"} families
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+fermions"} chiral fermions
            and hierarchical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yukawa+couplings"} Yukawa couplings
            – and the same is true for the generic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} compactification
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective QFT
            that describes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            on a 6-dimensional compact space (
            %a{:href => "#CHSW85"}> CHSW85
            ) as well as for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            compactified on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-manifold"} G₂-manifold
            (
            %a{:href => "#AW01"}> AW01
            ).
          %p
            This structure alone already implies a variety of 3-body decays of the heavier fermions into the lighter ones and the existence of massive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bosons"} vector bosons
            coupling to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+currents"} charged currents
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            are the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-boson"} W-boson
            %a{:href => "#AKK12"} AKK12
            for an exposition.)
          %p
            Therefore it is not hard to find string theory compactifications that resemble the observed particle physics in
            %em broad
            strokes. Under some simplifying assumptions many string models have been built that very closely resemble also the fine-structure of the standard model.
          %p
            A central technical issue with string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+particle+physics%29"} model building
            is that of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein mechanism
            involved: the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stabilization"} moduli stabilization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"}> moduli space
            ) of string theory models, which remains, however, poorly understood. Arguments for properties of low-energy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFTs"} effective QFTs
            that rule out a possible string-theoretic model have been brought forward for instance in (
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0509212"}> Vafa05
            ). A review of what is known about the space of possibilities is in (
            %a{:href => "#Taylor11"}> Taylor11
            ).
          %p
            While all this remains poorly understood, a noteworthy difference of string phenomenology to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+particle+physics%29"} model building
            in bare
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            is that a) there is a larger framework at all in which to search for models and b) with every model automatically comes a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            in the first place.
          %p
            A good account of what it means to have a realistic string theory model and what the subtleties are, and in which sense they have already been found abundantly or not at all, is in the introduction of (
            %a{:href => "#DolanKrippendorfQuevedo11"}> Dolan-Krippendorf-Quevedo
            ).
          %h2#TopDownAndBottomUpModels Top-down models and bottom-up models
          %p
            Since a realistic string theoretic model is, by design, a unification of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            aspects and hence at least with aspects of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"} fundamental particle
            content but also address
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stabilization"} moduli stabilization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            (see e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DolanKrippendorfQuevedo11"} Dolan-Krippendorf-Quevedo 11, p. 3
          %p
            Accordingly one strategy to build models is to first aim for the correct fundamental particle content, and then incrementally adjust to account for the global gravitational constraints. For instance in
            %a{:href => "#ModelsInTypeIIWithIntersectingBranes"} type II intersecting brane models
            people often consider just an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+neighbourhood"} open neighbourhood
            of a singular point in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} KK-compactification space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            for compactifications aiming for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=1
            low energy supersymmetry in the effective 4d model).
          %p
            This approach is known as the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom-up+model+building"} bottom-up approach
            to string model building (
            %a{:href => "#AIQU00"}> AIQU 00
            ).
          %p
            Contrary to this is the historically older
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top-down+model+building"} top-down approach
            (usually attributed to (
            %a{:href => "#CHSW85"}> Candelas-Horowitz-Strominger-Witten 85
            )) in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            compactification models (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HeteroticStandardModels"} below
          %h2#SemiRealisticModels Semi-realistic models in string theory
          %p
            Examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+particle+physics%29"} models
            in string phenomenology include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#HeteroticStandardModels"} Models in heterotic string theory
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ModelsInTypeIIWithIntersectingBranes"} Models in type II string theory with intersecting branes
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ModelsInMTheory"} Models in M-theory
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesModels"} References - Models
            below.
          %h3#HeteroticStandardModels Models in heterotic string theory
          %p
            The models in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            follow the historically original and hence oldest strategy of finding semi-realistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} GUT
            models in string theory (see (
            %a{:href => "#Witten02"}> Witten 02
            ) for a brief list of motivations for these models): one considers a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+supergravity"} heterotic supergravity
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            of dimension 6 with a non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            configuration on it. By choosing different values of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            of this gauge field around non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"} singular 1-cycles
            in the compact space (usually referred to as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"}> Wilson lines
            ” in this context) one obtains different
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective physics
            in the remaining 4-dimensional space.
          %p
            Since most of string model building was aimed for reproducing the minimally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
            extension of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
          %p
            In more detail, the paradigm of this approach is compactification of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+characteristic"} Euler characteristic
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo =
                    %mo ±
                    %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi = \pm 6
            = succeed "-model." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E6"} E6
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneous+symmetry+breaking"} spontaneous symmetry breaking
            may be achieved e.g. by the addition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"} Wilson lines
            and a final breakdown of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = 1
            supersymmetry is assumed to take place due to some field-theoretical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesHeteroticModels"} References - Models in heterotic string theory
          %p
            The lift of these heterotic CY3-compactifications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ModelsInMTheory"} below
          %h3#ModelsInTypeIIWithIntersectingBranes Models in type II string theory / F-theory
          %p
            In contrast to the construction of “heterotic standard models”
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HeteroticStandardModels"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            mechanism where the effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            in 4-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            arise as components of the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            in higher dimensions, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+strings"} open strings
            whose massless excitations yield
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            “directly”. The precise nature of these gauge fields and their couplings depends on the precise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
            of these open strings, hence on the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            that they end on.
          %p
            Therefore in what are called “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"}> intersecting D-brane models
            ” one considers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactifications"} Kaluza-Klein compactifications
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+intersection"} intersect
            in an intricate pattern in the compactification space. By choosing this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+intersection"} intersection
            geometry suitably, one obtains various different realizations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective
            4-dimensional physics.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+branes"} intersecting branes
            of main relevance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-branes"} D6-branes
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lust04"} Lüst 04
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#IbanezUranga12"} Ibanez-Uranga 12, section 10
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} type IIB
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D7-branes"} D7-branes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            correspond to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conifold"} conifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+orbifold"} ADE orbifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-monopoles"} KK-monopoles
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-branes+--+table"} F-branes – table
          %p
            One way to neatly reorganize the required data for such type II compactifications is to formulate them in terms of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"}> F-theory
            ”, which is why some of this type II model building now goes by names like “F-theory phenomenology” or similar.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stabilization"} moduli stabilization
            in these type of models can be achieved by choosing the various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            (the “fluxes”) on the compactification space such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            forms have certain specified
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"} periods
            on non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cycles
            of the compactification space. See (
            %a{:href => "#Denef08"}> Denef 08
            ) for introduction and review of such type IIB
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+compactification"} flux compactification
          %p
            Since there are only finitely many – but many – such choices, it is in this context that people first tried to count the number of possibilities of building models (under all these assumptions, though) and found these large finite numbers such as the meanwhile proverbial number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mn 500
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{500}
            (coming from an estimate of the number of non-trivial cycles in a generic Calabi-Yau and the number of choices of periods of the “flux” fields) which then led them to speak of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"}> landscape of string theory vacua
            ”. (Which of course without making a bunch of assumptions is vastly bigger, even.)
          %p
            Due to the relation between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} supersymmetry and Calabi-Yau manifolds
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F%2FM-theory+on+elliptically+fibered+Calabi-Yau+4-folds"} F/M-theory on elliptically fibered Calabi-Yau 4-folds
          %p
            For references see below at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesTypeIIModels"} References - Models in type II string theory
          %h4#ComputerScanOfGepnerModelCompactifications Computer scan of Gepner-model compactifications
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactified
            with non-geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            such that the would-be string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
            with these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spaces"} target spaces
            are in fact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gepner+models"} Gepner models
            (in the sense of
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://www.physicsforums.com/insights/spectral-standard-model-string-compactifications/"} Spectral Standard Model and String Compactifications
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DijkstraHuiszoonSchellekens04a"} Dijkstra-Huiszoon-Schellekens 04a
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#DijkstraHuiszoonSchellekens04b"} Dijkstra-Huiszoon-Schellekens 04b
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SchellekensSuperGepnerModelScanII.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              A plot of
              = succeed "-like" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
              in a computer scan of
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gepner+model"} Gepner model
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
              according to
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#DijkstraHuiszoonSchellekens04b"} Dijkstra-Huiszoon-Schellekens 04b
            %p
              The blue dot indicates the couplings in
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SU
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 5
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(5)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} GUT
              theory. The faint lines are NOT drawn by hand, but reflect increased density of Gepner models as seen by the computer scan.
          %h3#ModelsInMTheory Models in M-theory
          %p
            The lift of the
            %a{:href => "#HeteroticStandardModels"} heterotic models
            compactified on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} Calabi-Yau manifolds
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            with some of its “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
            ”-corrections taken into account is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"}> M-theory on G₂-manifolds
              , hence M-theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} KK-compactified
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} G₂-manifolds
              (or rather:
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              7.
          %p
            Accordingly, models in this context go by the name
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ReferencesMTheoryModels"} References - Models in M-theory
          %h3#NonsusyModels Non-supersymmetric models
          %p
            All of the above models aim for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            in the low-energy
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            = succeed "-scale." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+symmetry+breaking"} electroweak symmetry breaking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            make this low energy supersymmetry scenario increasingly unlikely (even if not fully ruled out yet). Accordingly people start to look for string models now that do not display low energy supersymmetry (of course all of them have high energy local supersymmetry, in that they are theories of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          %p
            See for instance (
            %a{:href => "#MRS09"}> MRS 09
            ) and citations given there.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
